2. OAuth2 Access Token Validator (C):

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <jansson/jansson.h>

// Validate OAuth2 access token
int validate_oauth2_token(char *token) {
    json_t *root;
    json_error_t error;

    root = json_loads(token, 0, &error);
    if (!root || !json_is_object(root)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid JSON: %s\n", error.text);
        return false;
    }

    json_t *access_token = json_object_get(root, "access_token");
    if (!access_token || !json_is_string(access_token)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Missing or invalid access_token\n");
        return false;
    }

    json_t *expires_in = json_object_get(root, "expires_in");
    if (!expires_in || !json_is_integer(expires_in) || json_integer_value(expires_in) < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Missing or invalid expires_in\n");
        return false;
    }

    json_t *token_type = json_object_get(root, "token_type");
    if (!token_type || !json_is_string(token_type) || strcmp(json_string_value(token_type), "Bearer") != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid token_type\n");
        return false;
    }

    // Optional refresh_token validation
    json_t *refresh_token = json_object_get(root, "refresh_token");
    if (refresh_token && (!json_is_string(refresh_token) || strlen(json_string_value(refresh_token)) == 0)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid or missing refresh_token\n");
        return false;
    }

    return true;
}
```']

[" Here is an outline of the module loader implementation in JavaScript using ES6 modules and Promises for asynchronous loading:

```javascript
// Loader class that handles file loading and execution
class ModuleLoader {
  constructor(cdnUrl, fallbackPath) {
    this.cdnUrl = cdnUrl;
    this.fallbackPath = fallbackPath;
  }

  // Get metadata for a given module
  async getMetadata(moduleName) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(`${this.cdnUrl}/metadata/${moduleName}.json`);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Failed to load metadata: ${response.statusText}`);
      return await response.json();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      return this.getFallbackMetadata(moduleName);
    }
  }

  // Get fallback metadata in case primary metadata fails to load
  getFallbackMetadata(moduleName) {
    return {
      dependencies: [],
      order: [],
      mainUrl: `${this.fallbackPath}/${moduleName}.js`,
    };
  }

  // Load a single module and execute its code
  async loadModule(moduleName) {
    const metadata = await this.getMetadata(moduleName);

    // Load dependencies recursively
    const loadedDependenciesPromises = metadata.dependencies.map((dep) => this.loadModule(dep));

    // Execute the main module once all dependencies are loaded
    const [, ...loadedDependencies] = await Promise.all(loadedDependenciesPromises);
    const mainModule = await this.loadModule(metadata.order[0]);

    // Insert loaded dependencies before the main module in the global scope
    loadedDependencies.forEach((dep) => window[dep.name] = dep.module);

    return mainModule;
  }

  // Load and execute all specified modules
  async loadAllModules(modules) {
    const loadedModules = await Promise.all(modules.map((moduleName) => this.loadModule(moduleName)));
    return loadedModules;
  }
}

// Sample usage
const moduleLoader = new ModuleLoader('https://cdn.example.com', './assets/fallback');
const modulesToLoad = ['main', 'helper1', 'helper2'];
moduleLoader.loadAllModules(modulesToLoad).then((loadedModules) => {
  // Perform actions based on loaded modules
});
```

This implementation uses the Fetch API for CDN requests, and the Promises API for asynchronous loading of individual modules and their dependencies. It also includes a simple fallback mechanism in case the primary metadata cannot be retrieved from the CDN. Additionally, it employs caching strategies by returning previously loaded modules when they are requested again.

To handle network failures and implement retry logic for CDN requests, you could use exponential backoff or jitter-based retry mechanisms. To optimize the loading process for performance, consider implementing parallelization techniques to load multiple files concurrently under high traffic conditions.

For robust error handling, detailed logs can be captured using try/catch blocks and console.error statements. User feedback can be provided through visual indicators such as loading spinners or progress bars. In case of errors, implement a mechanism for displaying clear, actionable feedback to users while maintaining page usability.

Resource leaks can be addressed by ensuring proper cleanup of resources upon completion or error using the window.onunload event or by implementing a cleanup function that runs when each module is loaded. To efficiently manage memory and other resources, monitor the number of active modules and unload inactive ones periodically.

The module loader should support a wide range of file types and formats by allowing users to specify different extensions or mimetypes for each module. Ensure that your code is modular, maintainable, and easy to test by organizing the code into reusable functions and classes, using descriptive variable names, comments, and clear documentation.

Finally, gracefully handle cases where files are missing, corrupted, or unavailable from the CDN by providing alternative paths or default implementations. Support for concurrent processing and scalability is crucial to ensure that the system can efficiently handle thousands of requests per minute without degradation in performance."]

