[' I will provide you with the basic structure of the `CombatSystem` class in C++ using the Boost.Asio library for handling concurrency, networking, and asynchronous operations. For simplicity, I am using a simple pseudo-random number generator to mimic the weighted probability distribution. The external API integration is not included due to its specifics being unclear from the question.

```cpp
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <random>
#include <chrono>
#include <map>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>

class CombatSystem {
public:
    // Constructor and destructor implementation omitted for brevity

    void performAttack() {
        std::random_device rd;
        std::mt19937 gen(rd());
        std::uniform_int_distribution<> distrib(0, 100);

        auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();

        int choice = distrib(gen);
        double probability[] = { 0.3, 0.5, 0.2 };
        if (choice <= probability[0] * 100) {
            attackIndex = 0;
        } else if (choice > probability[0] * 100 && choice <= probability[0] * 100 + probability[1] * 100) {
            attackIndex = 1;
        } else {
            attackIndex = 2;
        }

        auto nextAttackTime = cooldowns[attackIndex].load() + attackCooldownTimes[attackIndex];
        if (nextAttackTime > now) {
            // Cooldown period not yet passed, cannot perform another attack
            return;
        }

        // Perform the actual attack logic here

        cooldowns[attackIndex] = now;
        logAttackDetails();
        synchronizeWithClients();
    }

    void reset() {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < cooldownTimes.size(); ++i) {
            cooldowns[i].store(std::chrono::steady_clock::now());
        }
    }

private:
    void logAttackDetails() const {
        // Implement logging the attack details here
    }

    void synchronizeWithClients() {
        // Implement the logic for synchronizing with other clients here.
        // This could include network communication, data serialization/deserialization, and handling potential inconsistencies.
    }

    std::map<size_t, std::atomic<std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point>> cooldowns;
    std::vector<size_t> attackCooldownTimes = { 5 * std::chrono::seconds(1), 8 * std::chrono::seconds(1), 10 * std::chrono::seconds(1) };
    size_t attackIndex = 0;
};
```

This code only provides a basic outline for the `CombatSystem` class. It does not cover all the constraints mentioned in the question, but it serves as a starting point. You will need to expand this implementation by adding network communication, error handling for failed API requests and network disconnections, resource management, and performance optimizations.

Unit tests should be written to verify the correctness of the attack sequence and cooldown logic, as well as the robustness of the synchronization mechanism under varying network conditions.']

