7. `main.cpp`: Entry point of the application.

Key components:
- `ConfigManager`: Handles parsing configuration files and environment variables.
- `DataExtractor`: Abstract class for data extraction tasks, with specific implementations for each data source (HTTP APIs, message queues, databases).
- `AuthenticationProvider`: Manages authentication for legacy systems.
- `DataProcessor`: Handles concurrent data processing using thread pools and smart pointers.
- `ErrorHandler`: Implements robust error handling and recovery mechanisms.
- `Logger`: Provides logging and monitoring functionality.
- `ResourceBundle`: Loads a resource bundle from a specified file, manages memory dynamically, and returns the number of items in the bundle.

Here's an example implementation for the `ResourceBundle` function:

```cpp
#ifndef RESOURCE_BUNDLE_H
#define RESOURCE_BUNDLE_H

#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

struct ResourceItem {
    std::string data;
};

class ResourceBundle {
public:
    explicit ResourceBundle(const std::string& filename);
    ~ResourceBundle();
    size_t getSize() const;
    const ResourceItem& operator[](size_t index) const;

private:
    std::ifstream m_file;
    std::vector<ResourceItem> m_items;
};

// Implementation of the class methods
inline ResourceBundle::ResourceBundle(const std::string& filename)
    : m_file(filename, std::ios::in) {
    if (!m_file.is_open()) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to open file");
    }
}

inline ResourceBundle::~ResourceBundle() {
    m_file.close();
}

inline size_t ResourceBundle::getSize() const {
    return m_items.size();
}

inline const ResourceItem& ResourceBundle::operator[](size_t index) const {
    if (index >= m_items.size()) {
        throw std::out_of_range("Index out of range");
    }
    return m_items[index];
}
```']

